---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { ScdTimelineLab } from '../../../../components/english/ScdTimelineLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import setupSql from '../../../../features/english-scd/sql/setup.sql?raw'
import loadSql from '../../../../features/english-scd/sql/load.sql?raw'
import historySql from '../../../../features/english-scd/sql/history.sql?raw'
import scdStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-scd.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'SCD Type 2 SQL Query Example (PostgreSQL) | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Run a complete SCD Type 2 SQL query in PostgreSQL: detect customer changes, UPDATE old rows, INSERT new versions, and query history safely across retries.'
const completeSql = [setupSql, loadSql, historySql].join('\n')
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[scdStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'SCD Type 2' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · dimension history</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      SCD Type 2 SQL query: effective dates and historical rows
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      Slowly changing dimension Type 2 keeps one row per version of a business entity. Each row
      carries an effective date range and a current flag, so a historical fact can join to the
      attributes that were true on its own business date.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      On an attribute change, close the old row and insert a new one. Use the half-open range{' '}
      <code>[effective_from, effective_to)</code>: the start date belongs to the new version, the
      end date does not. Keep a stable business key (<code>customer_id</code>) plus a surrogate key
      (<code>customer_sk</code>) that identifies one version, and a current flag for the latest
      loaded version.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <p>
      Jump to the <a href="#sql-example">complete PostgreSQL example</a>, the{' '}
      <a href="#query">historical JOIN</a>, or the <a href="#faq">SQL FAQ</a>.
    </p>
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="example">
      <h2 id="example">Before and after: customer C-100 moves from Standard to Premium</h2>
      <p>
        Customer <code>C-100</code> is Standard / Austin in 2025. On <code>2026-03-01</code> the
        level becomes Premium and the branch becomes Denver. Loan <code>LN-77</code> was disbursed
        on <code>2025-11-15</code>. Move the date cursor, switch between overwrite and version rows,
        and watch which attributes the historical loan reads.
      </p>
      <ScdTimelineLab client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="rows">
      <h2 id="rows">The rows an SCD Type 2 update writes</h2>
      <p>
        The update touches two rows. The old row is closed, and a new row is inserted. Nothing is
        deleted, so the history remains queryable:
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Customer dimension after the 2026-03-01 change</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">customer_sk</th>
              <th scope="col">customer_id</th>
              <th scope="col">level</th>
              <th scope="col">branch</th>
              <th scope="col">effective_from</th>
              <th scope="col">effective_to</th>
              <th scope="col">is_current</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>1001</td>
              <td>C-100</td>
              <td>Standard</td>
              <td>Austin</td>
              <td>2025-01-01</td>
              <td>2026-03-01</td>
              <td>false</td>
            </tr>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>2002</td>
              <td>C-100</td>
              <td>Premium</td>
              <td>Denver</td>
              <td>2026-03-01</td>
              <td>open</td>
              <td>true</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>customer_id</code> stays the same because it identifies the customer.{' '}
        <code>customer_sk</code> changes because it identifies one version of that customer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="boundary">
      <h2 id="boundary">Boundary semantics: why the range is half-open</h2>
      <p>
        <code>effective_from</code> is the first business date on which a version is valid
        (inclusive). <code>effective_to</code> is the first date on which it is no longer valid
        (exclusive). <code>is_current</code> marks the latest loaded, open-ended version. In this
        example, <code>9999-12-31</code> is the open-end sentinel, not an actual planned change
        date; supported business dates are earlier than that sentinel.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>2026-02-28</strong> is before the new row’s <code>effective_from</code>, so it
          matches the old version: Standard / Austin.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>2026-03-01</strong> is the change date. Because the start is inclusive, it matches
          the new version: Premium / Denver.
        </li>
        <li>
          The old row ends at <code>2026-03-01</code>, but the end is exclusive, so the two versions
          never overlap.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="warning">
        <strong>Pick one convention and enforce it</strong>
        <p>
          Mixing inclusive and exclusive end dates creates either gaps or overlaps. Overlaps are
          worse: a historical fact can join to two dimension rows at once and duplicate the fact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="sql-example">
      <h2 id="sql-example">SCD Type 2 SQL Query Example</h2>
      <p>
        This complete example uses <strong>PostgreSQL 15+</strong>, not portable SQL for every
        database. Run it once in a fresh SQL session with autocommit enabled (for example, in{' '}
        <code>psql</code>). All tables are temporary and disappear on disconnect. The{' '}
        <code>customer</code> dimension represents the Banking Teaching Domain’s Customer: one
        business identity, one row per attribute version. The tracked attributes are customer level
        and branch; the scenario is the same C-100 change shown in the timeline.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Create the dimension, seed Standard / Austin, and stage Premium / Denver.</li>
        <li>
          Compare staged attributes to the current row with <code>IS DISTINCT FROM</code>, which
          also detects changes to or from <code>NULL</code>. A missing customer is a first load.
        </li>
        <li>
          Save the candidates before the <code>UPDATE</code>, close old versions, then{' '}
          <code>INSERT</code> the candidates as new current versions in the same transaction.
        </li>
        <li>Read all history and the version effective on the change date.</li>
      </ol>
      <pre
        data-scd-sql="complete"
        tabindex="0"
        role="region"
        aria-label="Complete PostgreSQL SCD Type 2 example"
      ><code>{completeSql}</code></pre>
      <h3 id="sql-result">Expected history and repeat execution</h3>
      <p>
        The first result contains exactly two rows: Standard / Austin over{' '}
        <code>[2025-01-01, 2026-03-01)</code> with <code>is_current = false</code>, then Premium /
        Denver over <code>[2026-03-01, 9999-12-31)</code> with <code>is_current = true</code>. In
        this fresh session the generated keys are 1001 and 1002; the timeline’s illustrative new key
        2002 has the same version-key role. The second result returns only Premium / Denver.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test a retry, rerun only the block from <code>-- LOAD START</code> through{' '}
        <code>-- LOAD END</code> in the same session, without rerunning setup. Both the{' '}
        <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>INSERT</code> write zero rows: the staged attributes already
        match the current version. A later batch with the same attributes also creates no version.
        To test a first load, start a fresh session and omit the initial{' '}
        <code>INSERT INTO customer</code>; the same loader inserts one current row into the empty
        dimension. A new customer in staging follows that same path.
      </p>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="warning">
        <strong>A forward-only teaching loader, not a full CDC pipeline</strong>
        <p>
          Stage at most one row per customer per batch and process batches in effective-date order.
          A changed payload at the same or an earlier start date is rejected rather than creating a
          zero-length or overlapping version. After an error, issue <code>ROLLBACK</code> before
          retrying. A missing staged customer is not a deletion. Late corrections need explicit
          range repair; timestamps are needed for multiple distinct changes within a day.
        </p>
        <p>
          The transaction makes closing and inserting atomic. The table lock serializes writers when
          adapted to a shared dimension, at the cost of write concurrency. Uniqueness and check
          constraints provide guardrails, but do not prevent every possible historical overlap from
          arbitrary external writes. All writers must follow the loading contract. With future-dated
          versions, the current flag means latest loaded, not necessarily valid today; use an as-of
          date predicate instead.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        This is a dimension-specific application of{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>idempotent ETL</a>. For choosing which source
        records reach staging, see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('incrementalVsFullLoad')}>incremental vs full load</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="query">
      <h2 id="query">The historical query pattern</h2>
      <p>
        The fact table stores the business date (or timestamp) that determines the version. Join the
        fact to the dimension row whose range contains that date:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`SELECT l.note_id,
       l.disbursed_date,
       c.customer_sk,
       c.level,
       c.branch
FROM loan_note AS l
JOIN customer AS c
  ON c.customer_id = l.customer_id
 AND l.disbursed_date >= c.effective_from
 AND l.disbursed_date <  c.effective_to;

-- customer_sk 1001 → Standard / Austin for a 2025-11-15 disbursement`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The latest loaded row is the one with <code>is_current = true</code> (or the row whose{' '}
        <code>effective_to</code> is the open sentinel). Do not use the current flag for historical
        analysis — it selects the latest version, not necessarily “what was true then”. A{' '}
        <code>BETWEEN</code> predicate includes both endpoints, so it is not suitable for these
        half-open ranges.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="strategies">
      <h2 id="strategies">Type 1, Type 2 and when each fits</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Dimension history strategies</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Strategy</th>
              <th scope="col">What it keeps</th>
              <th scope="col">Use when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Type 1</strong> — overwrite
              </td>
              <td>One row, current attributes only</td>
              <td>History does not matter (for example fixing a typo)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>
                <strong>Type 2</strong> — version rows
              </td>
              <td>Every version with effective dates</td>
              <td>Historical facts must be interpreted with the attributes of their time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Type 3</strong> — previous value column
              </td>
              <td>Current and one previous value</td>
              <td>Only the last change is ever needed</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="edge-cases">
      <h2 id="edge-cases">Edge cases and trade-offs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Several changes on the same day.</strong> The range must still be unambiguous.
          Decide whether same-day changes collapse into one version or use timestamps instead of
          dates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Late corrections to history.</strong> A backdated change may require splitting an
          existing version. Treat it as a new version with explicit effective dates, not an in-place
          edit. {'The corrected range also affects downstream tables; see '}
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('lineageVsTaskDependency')}>data lineage vs task dependency</a>
          {' for how to bound that impact.'}
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Only version what needs history.</strong> Every versioned attribute adds rows and
          join complexity. Keep attributes that nobody queries historically in the current row only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Query cost.</strong> Range joins benefit from an index on{' '}
          <code>(customer_id, effective_from)</code>. The current flag alone is not enough for
          historical queries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unknown values.</strong> Facts can reference a customer that did not exist yet at
          the business date. Decide whether to use an “unknown” dimension row or reject the fact.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="faq">
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3 id="faq-merge">Should I use MERGE for SCD Type 2 SQL?</h3>
      <p>
        <code>MERGE</code> can help express source-to-target matching, but it does not automatically
        implement Type 2 history. A simple PostgreSQL 15 <code>WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE</code>{' '}
        action closes a matched row; that same source row does not also run a{' '}
        <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT</code> action. This example uses explicit{' '}
        <code>UPDATE</code> plus <code>INSERT</code> over saved candidates so both writes are
        visible and atomic. Other databases and versions have different MERGE semantics; adapt and
        test rather than pasting this PostgreSQL script unchanged.
      </p>
      <h3 id="faq-history">How do I query a customer’s historical version?</h3>
      <p>
        Filter on <code>customer_id</code> and{' '}
        <code>business_date &gt;= effective_from AND business_date &lt; effective_to</code>. For
        C-100, 2026-02-28 returns Standard / Austin; 2026-03-01 returns Premium / Denver. The{' '}
        <a href="#query">historical JOIN pattern</a> applies that same condition to each loan’s
        disbursement date. Filtering only on <code>is_current</code> loses that history; overlapping
        ranges can also cause <a href={getEnglishRoute('joinDuplicateRows')}>duplicate JOIN rows</a>
        .
      </p>
      <h3 id="faq-retry">Will rerunning the SQL create duplicate versions?</h3>
      <p>
        Not when the same staged batch is immediately retried through the complete load transaction:
        unchanged attributes produce no candidates, so no old row is closed and no new row is
        inserted. The unique current-row index and <code>(customer_id, effective_from)</code>{' '}
        constraint add protection. They are not a substitute for the change comparison or
        transaction. Replaying an older batch after newer changes is different: this forward-only
        example rejects changed backdated payloads; use a separate correction/backfill policy for
        that case. The setup is a one-time session fixture, not the retry block.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a>
          <p>Why “one row per version” is a grain decision, not a column choice.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>How to make an ETL job idempotent</a>
          <p>Loading dimension versions safely across retries and backfills.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('lineageVsTaskDependency')}>Data lineage vs task dependency</a>
          <p>Finding the downstream tables a corrected history affects.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
